# Responsive First

Design and implement UI for every viewport from the start. Do not ship desktop-only layouts and patch mobile later.

## Requirements

- Support phones, tablets, laptops, and large desktops.
- Prefer mobile-first Tailwind breakpoints (`sm:`, `md:`, `lg:`, `xl:`) over desktop-first overrides.
- Layouts must reflow: stacks on small screens, multi-column only when space allows.
- Touch targets stay usable on mobile (adequate tap size and spacing).
- Avoid horizontal scroll on common viewports unless the content itself requires it (e.g. wide tables — then provide a clear overflow pattern).
- Test mentally at ~375px, ~768px, and ~1280px before finishing UI work.
- Prefer fluid units (`%`, `fr`, `minmax`, container queries when already in use) over fixed pixel widths for page chrome.

## Examples

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — desktop-only grid
<div className="grid grid-cols-4 gap-4">...</div>

// ✅ GOOD — mobile first
<div className="grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 md:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4">...</div>
```

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — fixed width breaks small screens
<aside className="w-[320px]">...</aside>

// ✅ GOOD
<aside className="w-full md:w-64 lg:w-80">...</aside>
```

## Browser Support

- Stick to patterns already used in this project (modern evergreen browsers).
- Do not rely on bleeding-edge CSS without a fallback already present in the codebase.
